{"id": "q0467", "pool": "frozen", "arm": "local", "tier": "never", "lang": "en", "run_id": "ttfa1", "attempt": 1, "seed": 1682517937, "protocol": "ttfa-v3", "config_sha256": "923cbbdfe3213927dfa36b1f8e0eb48a30d641d9b9bf7cf25d16765340d88836", "gate_art_sha256": "0e6494c2eeac9bcd86c10b5def3cbd32e98bb0765fa2fd8afc8c1b47915ea372", "threshold": null, "gpu": "NVIDIA H100 80GB HBM3", "host": "modal", "shard": 0, "query": "What are some cardio machines at the gym?", "input_path": "/data/audio_pool/q0467.wav", "input_sha256": "a348a140bd17f1135123fedb6faf5f2e340311b8be04fb503e92e3c28e12c144", "input_samples": 39400, "input_s": 2.462, "n_real_chunks": 3, "score": 0.2155, "act": 0.9951, "is_info": true, "fired": false, "mode": "local", "answer": "Well, there are a few different cardio machines at the gym. You have treadmills where you walk or run on a moving belt. There's also elliptical s, which move your legs in an elliptical motion to simulate running without the impact. Then there's stationary bikes, like spin classes or regular exercise bikes, where you pedal while sitting down. Some gyms even have row ing machines that work your upper body and legs simultaneously. And if you're into water workouts, they might offer underwater treadmills or swim lanes for lap swimming. Each one offers a unique way to get your heart rate up and stay active!", "relay_text": "", "expert_answer": "", "transcript": "", "asr_s": null, "expert_s": null, "expert_input_samples": null, "expert_input_s": null, "expert_input_sha256": null, "expert_timed_out": false, "wait_s": null, "relay_synth_ms": null, "onset_chunk": 3, "eot_seen": true, "n_speak_chunks": 17, "feed_lag_max_ms": 0, "feed_lag_sum_ms": 0, "audio_files": {"local": {"path": "/data/ttfa_real/ttfa1/frozen/audio/q0467_local_local.wav", "sha256": "ca460ef7674cb610ad8d572a8f7df726f22bca36d0aa82ce31e5dbd8f6880c3b", "sr": 24000, "seconds": 16.32}}, "chunks": [[0, 1, 1.0, 1.056, 1.1, 1, 0, 0], [1, 1, 2.0, 2.062, 2.111, 1, 0, 0], [2, 1, 2.462, 